<body>
<p align=3D"CENTER">
<b>BERKSHIRE HATHAWAY INC.</b>
</p>
<pre>
<i>To the Shareholders of Berkshire Hathaway Inc.:</i>

     Operating earnings improved to $41.9 million in 1980 from=20
$36.0 million in 1979, but return on beginning equity capital=20
(with securities valued at cost) fell to 17.8% from 18.6%. We=20
believe the latter yardstick to be the most appropriate measure=20
of single-year managerial economic performance.  Informed use of=20
that yardstick, however, requires an understanding of many=20
factors, including accounting policies, historical carrying=20
values of assets, financial leverage, and industry conditions.

     In your evaluation of our economic performance, we suggest=20
that two factors should receive your special attention - one of a=20
positive nature peculiar, to a large extent, to our own=20
operation, and one of a negative nature applicable to corporate=20
performance generally.  Let=92s look at the bright side first.

<i>Non-Controlled Ownership Earnings</i>

     When one company owns part of another company, appropriate=20
accounting procedures pertaining to that ownership interest must=20
be selected from one of three major categories.  The percentage=20
of voting stock that is owned, in large part, determines which=20
category of accounting principles should be utilized.

     Generally accepted accounting principles require (subject to=20
exceptions, naturally, as with our former bank subsidiary) full=20
consolidation of sales, expenses, taxes, and earnings of business=20
holdings more than 50% owned.  Blue Chip Stamps, 60% owned by=20
Berkshire Hathaway Inc., falls into this category.  Therefore,=20
all Blue Chip income and expense items are included in full in=20
Berkshire=92s Consolidated Statement of Earnings, with the 40%=20
ownership interest of others in Blue Chip=92s net earnings=20
reflected in the Statement as a deduction for =93minority=20
interest=94.

     Full inclusion of underlying earnings from another class of=20
holdings, companies owned 20% to 50% (usually called=20
=93investees=94), also normally occurs.  Earnings from such companies=20
- for example, Wesco Financial, controlled by Berkshire but only=20
48% owned - are included via a one-line entry in the owner=92s=20
Statement of Earnings.  Unlike the over-50% category, all items=20
of revenue and expense are omitted; just the proportional share=20
of net income is included.  Thus, if Corporation A owns one-third=20
of Corporation B, one-third of B=92s earnings, whether or not=20
distributed by B, will end up in A=92s earnings.  There are some=20
modifications, both in this and the over-50% category, for=20
intercorporate taxes and purchase price adjustments, the=20
explanation of which we will save for a later day. (We know you=20
can hardly wait.)

     Finally come holdings representing less than 20% ownership=20
of another corporation=92s voting securities.  In these cases,=20
accounting rules dictate that the owning companies include in=20
their earnings only dividends received from such holdings. =20
Undistributed earnings are ignored.  Thus, should we own 10% of=20
Corporation X with earnings of $10 million in 1980, we would=20
report in our earnings (ignoring relatively minor taxes on=20
intercorporate dividends) either (a) $1 million if X declared the=20
full $10 million in dividends; (b) $500,000 if X paid out 50%, or=20
$5 million, in dividends; or (c) zero if X reinvested all=20
earnings.

     We impose this short - and over-simplified - course in=20
accounting upon you because Berkshire=92s concentration of=20
resources in the insurance field produces a corresponding=20
concentration of its assets in companies in that third (less than=20
20% owned) category.  Many of these companies pay out relatively=20
small proportions of their earnings in dividends.  This means=20
that only a small proportion of their current earning power is=20
recorded in our own current operating earnings.  But, while our=20
reported operating earnings reflect only the dividends received=20
from such companies, our economic well-being is determined by=20
their earnings, not their dividends.

     Our holdings in this third category of companies have=20
increased dramatically in recent years as our insurance business=20
has prospered and as securities markets have presented=20
particularly attractive opportunities in the common stock area. =20
The large increase in such holdings, plus the growth of earnings=20
experienced by those partially-owned companies, has produced an=20
unusual result; the part of =93our=94 earnings that these companies=20
retained last year (the part not paid to us in dividends)=20
exceeded the total reported annual operating earnings of=20
Berkshire Hathaway.  Thus, conventional accounting only allows=20
less than half of our earnings =93iceberg=94 to appear above the=20
surface, in plain view.  Within the corporate world such a result=20
is quite rare; in our case it is likely to be recurring.

     Our own analysis of earnings reality differs somewhat from=20
generally accepted accounting principles, particularly when those=20
principles must be applied in a world of high and uncertain rates=20
of inflation. (But it=92s much easier to criticize than to improve=20
such accounting rules.  The inherent problems are monumental.) We=20
have owned 100% of businesses whose reported earnings were not=20
worth close to 100 cents on the dollar to us even though, in an=20
accounting sense, we totally controlled their disposition. (The=20
=93control=94 was theoretical.  Unless we reinvested all earnings,=20
massive deterioration in the value of assets already in place=20
would occur.  But those reinvested earnings had no prospect of=20
earning anything close to a market return on capital.) We have=20
also owned small fractions of businesses with extraordinary=20
reinvestment possibilities whose retained earnings had an=20
economic value to us far in excess of 100 cents on the dollar.

     <i>The value to Berkshire Hathaway of retained earnings is not=20
determined by whether we own 100%, 50%, 20% or 1% of the=20
businesses in which they reside.  Rather, the value of those=20
retained earnings is determined by the use to which they are put=20
and the subsequent level of earnings produced by that usage.</i> =20
This is true whether we determine the usage, or whether managers=20
we did not hire - but did elect to join - determine that usage.=20
(It=92s the act that counts, not the actors.) And the value is in=20
no way affected by the inclusion or non-inclusion of those=20
retained earnings in our own reported operating earnings.  If a=20
tree grows in a forest partially owned by us, but we don=92t record=20
the growth in our financial statements, we still own part of the=20
tree.

     Our view, we warn you, is non-conventional.  But we would=20
rather have earnings for which we did not get accounting credit=20
put to good use in a 10%-owned company by a management we did not=20
personally hire, than have earnings for which we did get credit=20
put into projects of more dubious potential by another management=20
- even if we are that management.

     (We can=92t resist pausing here for a short commercial.  One=20
usage of retained earnings we often greet with special enthusiasm=20
when practiced by companies in which we have an investment=20
interest is repurchase of their own shares.  The reasoning is=20
simple: if a fine business is selling in the market place for far=20
less than intrinsic value, what more certain or more profitable=20
utilization of capital can there be than significant enlargement=20
of the interests of all owners at that bargain price?  The=20
competitive nature of corporate acquisition activity almost=20
guarantees the payment of a full - frequently more than full=20
price when a company buys the entire ownership of another=20
enterprise.  But the auction nature of security markets often=20
allows finely-run companies the opportunity to purchase portions=20
of their own businesses at a price under 50% of that needed to=20
acquire the same earning power through the negotiated acquisition=20
of another enterprise.)

<i>Long-Term Corporate Results</i>

     As we have noted, we evaluate single-year corporate=20
performance by comparing operating earnings to shareholders=92=20
equity with securities valued at cost.  Our long-term yardstick=20
of performance, however, includes all capital gains or losses,=20
realized or unrealized.  We continue to achieve a long-term=20
return on equity that considerably exceeds the average of our=20
yearly returns.  The major factor causing this pleasant result is=20
a simple one: the retained earnings of those non-controlled=20
holdings we discussed earlier have been translated into gains in=20
market value.

     Of course, this translation of retained earnings into market=20
price appreciation is highly uneven (it goes in reverse some=20
years), unpredictable as to timing, and unlikely to materialize=20
on a precise dollar-for-dollar basis.  And a silly purchase price=20
for a block of stock in a corporation can negate the effects of a=20
decade of earnings retention by that corporation.  But when=20
purchase prices are sensible, some long-term market recognition=20
of the accumulation of retained earnings almost certainly will=20
occur.  Periodically you even will receive some frosting on the=20
cake, with market appreciation far exceeding post-purchase=20
retained earnings.

     In the sixteen years since present management assumed=20
responsibility for Berkshire, book value per share with=20
insurance-held equities valued at market has increased from=20
$19.46 to $400.80, or 20.5% compounded annually. (You=92ve done=20
better: the value of the mineral content in the human body=20
compounded at 22% annually during the past decade.) It is=20
encouraging, moreover, to realize that our record was achieved=20
despite many mistakes.  The list is too painful and lengthy to=20
detail here.  But it clearly shows that a reasonably competitive=20
corporate batting average can be achieved in spite of a lot of=20
managerial strikeouts.

     Our insurance companies will continue to make large=20
investments in well-run, favorably-situated, non-controlled=20
companies that very often will pay out in dividends only small=20
proportions of their earnings.  Following this policy, we would=20
expect our long-term returns to continue to exceed the returns=20
derived annually from reported operating earnings.  Our=20
confidence in this belief can easily be quantified: if we were to=20
sell the equities that we hold and replace them with long-term=20
tax-free bonds, our reported operating earnings would rise=20
immediately by over $30 million annually.  Such a shift tempts us=20
not at all. =20

     So much for the good news.

<i>Results for Owners</i>

     Unfortunately, earnings reported in corporate financial=20
statements are no longer the dominant variable that determines=20
whether there are any real earnings for you, the owner.  For only=20
gains in purchasing power represent real earnings on investment. =20
If you (a) forego ten hamburgers to purchase an investment; (b)=20
receive dividends which, after tax, buy two hamburgers; and (c)=20
receive, upon sale of your holdings, after-tax proceeds that will=20
buy eight hamburgers, then (d) you have had no real income from=20
your investment, no matter how much it appreciated in dollars. =20
You may feel richer, but you won=92t eat richer.

     High rates of inflation create a tax on capital that makes=20
much corporate investment unwise - at least if measured by the=20
criterion of a positive real investment return to owners.  This=20
=93hurdle rate=94 the return on equity that must be achieved by a=20
corporation in order to produce any real return for its=20
individual owners - has increased dramatically in recent years. =20
The average tax-paying investor is now running up a down=20
escalator whose pace has accelerated to the point where his=20
upward progress is nil.

     For example, in a world of 12% inflation a business earning=20
20% on equity (which very few manage consistently to do) and=20
distributing it all to individuals in the 50% bracket is chewing=20
up their real capital, not enhancing it. (Half of the 20% will go=20
for income tax; the remaining 10% leaves the owners of the=20
business with only 98% of the purchasing power they possessed at=20
the start of the year - even though they have not spent a penny=20
of their =93earnings=94).  The investors in this bracket would=20
actually be better off with a combination of stable prices and=20
corporate earnings on equity capital of only a few per cent.

     Explicit income taxes alone, unaccompanied by any implicit=20
inflation tax, never can turn a positive corporate return into a=20
negative owner return. (Even if there were 90% personal income=20
tax rates on both dividends and capital gains, some real income=20
would be left for the owner at a zero inflation rate.) But the=20
inflation tax is not limited by reported income.  Inflation rates=20
not far from those recently experienced can turn the level of=20
positive returns achieved by a majority of corporations into=20
negative returns for all owners, including those not required to=20
pay explicit taxes. (For example, if inflation reached 16%,=20
owners of the 60% plus of corporate America earning less than=20
this rate of return would be realizing a negative real return -=20
even if income taxes on dividends and capital gains were=20
eliminated.)

     Of course, the two forms of taxation co-exist and interact=20
since explicit taxes are levied on nominal, not real, income. =20
Thus you pay income taxes on what would be deficits if returns to=20
stockholders were measured in constant dollars.

     At present inflation rates, we believe individual owners in=20
medium or high tax brackets (as distinguished from tax-free=20
entities such as pension funds, eleemosynary institutions, etc.)=20
should expect no real long-term return from the average American=20
corporation, even though these individuals reinvest the entire=20
after-tax proceeds from all dividends they receive.  The average=20
return on equity of corporations is fully offset by the=20
combination of the implicit tax on capital levied by inflation=20
and the explicit taxes levied both on dividends and gains in=20
value produced by retained earnings.

     As we said last year, Berkshire has no corporate solution to=20
the problem. (We=92ll say it again next year, too.) Inflation does=20
not improve our return on equity.

     Indexing is the insulation that all seek against inflation. =20
But the great bulk (although there are important exceptions) of=20
corporate capital is not even partially indexed.  Of course,=20
earnings and dividends per share usually will rise if significant=20
earnings are =93saved=94 by a corporation; i.e., reinvested instead=20
of paid as dividends.  But that would be true without inflation. =20
A thrifty wage earner, likewise, could achieve regular annual=20
increases in his total income without ever getting a pay increase=20
- <i>if</i> he were willing to take only half of his paycheck in cash=20
(his wage =93dividend=94) and consistently add the other half (his=20
=93retained earnings=94) to a savings account.  Neither this high-
saving wage earner nor the stockholder in a high-saving=20
corporation whose annual dividend rate increases while its rate=20
of return on equity remains flat is truly indexed.

     For capital to be truly indexed, return on equity must rise,=20
i.e., business earnings consistently must increase in proportion=20
to the increase in the price level <i>without any need for the=20
business to add to capital - including working capital -=20
employed.</i>  (Increased earnings produced by increased investment=20
don=92t count.) Only a few businesses come close to exhibiting this=20
ability.  And Berkshire Hathaway isn=92t one of them.

     We, of course, have a corporate policy of reinvesting=20
earnings for growth, diversity and strength, which has the=20
incidental effect of minimizing the current imposition of=20
explicit taxes on our owners.  However, on a day-by-day basis,=20
you will be subjected to the implicit inflation tax, and when you=20
wish to transfer your investment in Berkshire into another form=20
of investment, or into consumption, you also will face explicit=20
taxes.

