     From this table, you can see that our sources of underlying=20
earning power are distributed far differently among industries=20
than would superficially seem the case.  For example, our=20
insurance subsidiaries own approximately 3% of Kaiser Aluminum,=20
and 1 1/4% of Alcoa.  Our share of the 1980 earnings of those=20
companies amounts to about $13 million. (If translated dollar for=20
dollar into a combination of eventual market value gain and=20
dividends, this figure would have to be reduced by a significant,=20
but not precisely determinable, amount of tax; perhaps 25% would=20
be a fair assumption.) Thus, we have a much larger economic=20
interest in the aluminum business than in practically any of the=20
operating businesses we control and on which we report in more=20
detail.  If we maintain our holdings, our long-term performance=20
will be more affected by the future economics of the aluminum=20
industry than it will by direct operating decisions we make=20
concerning most companies over which we exercise managerial=20
control.

<i>GEICO Corp.</i>

     Our largest non-controlled holding is 7.2 million shares of=20
GEICO Corp., equal to about a 33% equity interest.  Normally, an=20
interest of this magnitude (over 20%) would qualify as an=20
=93investee=94 holding and would require us to reflect a=20
proportionate share of GEICO=92s earnings in our own.  However, we=20
purchased our GEICO stock pursuant to special orders of the=20
District of Columbia and New York Insurance Departments, which=20
required that the right to vote the stock be placed with an=20
independent party.  Absent the vote, our 33% interest does not=20
qualify for investee treatment. (Pinkerton=92s is a similar=20
situation.)=20

     Of course, whether or not the undistributed earnings of=20
GEICO are picked up annually in our operating earnings figure has=20
nothing to do with their economic value to us, or to you as=20
owners of Berkshire.  The value of these retained earnings will=20
be determined by the skill with which they are put to use by=20
GEICO management.

     On this score, we simply couldn=92t feel better.  GEICO=20
represents the best of all investment worlds - the coupling of a=20
very important and very hard to duplicate business advantage with=20
an extraordinary management whose skills in operations are=20
matched by skills in capital allocation.

     As you can see, our holdings cost us $47 million, with about=20
half of this amount invested in 1976 and most of the remainder=20
invested in 1980.  At the present dividend rate, our reported=20
earnings from GEICO amount to a little over $3 million annually. =20
But we estimate our share of its earning power is on the order of=20
$20 million annually.  Thus, undistributed earnings applicable to=20
this holding alone may amount to 40% of total reported operating=20
earnings of Berkshire.

     We should emphasize that we feel as comfortable with GEICO=20
management retaining an estimated $17 million of earnings=20
applicable to our ownership as we would if that sum were in our=20
own hands.  In just the last two years GEICO, through repurchases=20
of its own stock, has reduced the share equivalents it has=20
outstanding from 34.2 million to 21.6 million, dramatically=20
enhancing the interests of shareholders in a business that simply=20
can=92t be replicated.  The owners could not have been better=20
served.

     We have written in past reports about the disappointments=20
that usually result from purchase and operation of =93turnaround=94=20
businesses.  Literally hundreds of turnaround possibilities in=20
dozens of industries have been described to us over the years=20
and, either as participants or as observers, we have tracked=20
performance against expectations.  Our conclusion is that, with=20
few exceptions, when a management with a reputation for=20
brilliance tackles a business with a reputation for poor=20
fundamental economics, it is the reputation of the business that=20
remains intact.

     GEICO may appear to be an exception, having been turned=20
around from the very edge of bankruptcy in 1976.  It certainly is=20
true that managerial brilliance was needed for its resuscitation,=20
and that Jack Byrne, upon arrival in that year, supplied that=20
ingredient in abundance.

     But it also is true that the fundamental business advantage=20
that GEICO had enjoyed - an advantage that previously had=20
produced staggering success - was still intact within the=20
company, although submerged in a sea of financial and operating=20
troubles.

     GEICO was designed to be the low-cost operation in an=20
enormous marketplace (auto insurance) populated largely by=20
companies whose marketing structures restricted adaptation.  Run=20
as designed, it could offer unusual value to its customers while=20
earning unusual returns for itself.  For decades it had been run=20
in just this manner.  Its troubles in the mid-70s were not=20
produced by any diminution or disappearance of this essential=20
economic advantage.

     GEICO=92s problems at that time put it in a position analogous=20
to that of American Express in 1964 following the salad oil=20
scandal.  Both were one-of-a-kind companies, temporarily reeling=20
from the effects of a fiscal blow that did not destroy their=20
exceptional underlying economics.  The GEICO and American Express=20
situations, extraordinary business franchises with a localized=20
excisable cancer (needing, to be sure, a skilled surgeon), should=20
be distinguished from the true =93turnaround=94 situation in which=20
the managers expect - and need - to pull off a corporate=20
Pygmalion.

     Whatever the appellation, we are delighted with our GEICO=20
holding which, as noted, cost us $47 million.  To buy a similar=20
$20 million of earning power in a business with first-class=20
economic characteristics and bright prospects would cost a=20
minimum of $200 million (much more in some industries) if it had=20
to be accomplished through negotiated purchase of an entire=20
company.  A 100% interest of that kind gives the owner the=20
options of leveraging the purchase, changing managements,=20
directing cash flow, and selling the business.  It may also=20
provide some excitement around corporate headquarters (less=20
frequently mentioned).

     We find it perfectly satisfying that the nature of our=20
insurance business dictates we buy many minority portions of=20
already well-run businesses (at prices far below our share of the=20
total value of the entire business) that do not need management=20
change, re-direction of cash flow, or sale.  There aren=92t many=20
Jack Byrnes in the managerial world, or GEICOs in the business=20
world.  What could be better than buying into a partnership with=20
both of them?

<i>Insurance Industry Conditions</i>

     The insurance industry=92s underwriting picture continues to=20
unfold about as we anticipated, with the combined ratio (see=20
definition on page 37) rising from 100.6 in 1979 to an estimated=20
103.5 in 1980.  It is virtually certain that this trend will=20
continue and that industry underwriting losses will mount,=20
significantly and progressively, in 1981 and 1982.  To understand=20
why, we recommend that you read the excellent analysis of=20
property-casualty competitive dynamics done by Barbara Stewart of=20
Chubb Corp. in an October 1980 paper. (Chubb=92s annual report=20
consistently presents the most insightful, candid and well-
written discussion of industry conditions; you should get on the=20
company=92s mailing list.) Mrs. Stewart=92s analysis may not be=20
cheerful, but we think it is very likely to be accurate.

     And, unfortunately, a largely unreported but particularly=20
pernicious problem may well prolong and intensify the coming=20
industry agony.  It is not only likely to keep many insurers=20
scrambling for business when underwriting losses hit record=20
levels - it is likely to cause them at such a time to redouble=20
their efforts.

     This problem arises from the decline in bond prices and the=20
insurance accounting convention that allows companies to carry=20
bonds at amortized cost, regardless of market value.  Many=20
insurers own long-term bonds that, at amortized cost, amount to=20
two to three times net worth.  If the level is three times, of=20
course, a one-third shrink from cost in bond prices - if it were=20
to be recognized on the books - would wipe out net worth.  And=20
shrink they have.  Some of the largest and best known property-
casualty companies currently find themselves with nominal, or=20
even negative, net worth when bond holdings are valued at market. =20
Of course their bonds could rise in price, thereby partially, or=20
conceivably even fully, restoring the integrity of stated net=20
worth.  Or they could fall further. (We believe that short-term=20
forecasts of stock or bond prices are useless.  The forecasts may=20
tell you a great deal about the forecaster; they tell you nothing=20
about the future.)

     It might strike some as strange that an insurance company=92s=20
survival is threatened when its stock portfolio falls=20
sufficiently in price to reduce net worth significantly, but that=20
an even greater decline in bond prices produces no reaction at=20
all.  The industry would respond by pointing out that, no matter=20
what the current price, the bonds will be paid in full at=20
maturity, thereby eventually eliminating any interim price=20
decline.  It may take twenty, thirty, or even forty years, this=20
argument says, but, as long as the bonds don=92t have to be sold,=20
in the end they=92ll all be worth face value.  Of course, if they=20
are sold even if they are replaced with similar bonds offering=20
better relative value - the loss must be booked immediately. =20
And, just as promptly, published net worth must be adjusted=20
downward by the amount of the loss.

     Under such circumstances, a great many investment options=20
disappear, perhaps for decades.  For example, when large=20
underwriting losses are in prospect, it may make excellent=20
business logic for some insurers to shift from tax-exempt bonds=20
into taxable bonds.  Unwillingness to recognize major bond losses=20
may be the sole factor that prevents such a sensible move.

     But the full implications flowing from massive unrealized=20
bond losses are far more serious than just the immobilization of=20
investment intellect.  For the source of funds to purchase <i>and=20
hold</i> those bonds is a pool of money derived from policyholders=20
and claimants (with changing faces) - money which, in effect, is=20
temporarily on deposit with the insurer.  As long as this pool=20
retains its size, no bonds must be sold.  If the pool of funds=20
shrinks - which it will if the volume of business declines=20
significantly - assets must be sold to pay off the liabilities. =20
And if those assets consist of bonds with big unrealized losses,=20
such losses will rapidly become realized, decimating net worth in=20
the process.

     Thus, an insurance company with a bond market value=20
shrinkage approaching stated net worth (of which there are now=20
many) and also faced with inadequate rate levels that are sure to=20
deteriorate further has two options.  One option for management=20
is to tell the underwriters to keep pricing according to the=20
exposure involved - =93be sure to get a dollar of premium for every=20
dollar of expense cost plus expectable loss cost=94.

     The consequences of this directive are predictable: (a) with=20
most business both price sensitive and renewable annually, many=20
policies presently on the books will be lost to competitors in=20
rather short order; (b) as premium volume shrinks significantly,=20
there will be a lagged but corresponding decrease in liabilities=20
(unearned premiums and claims payable); (c) assets (bonds) must=20
be sold to match the decrease in liabilities; and (d) the=20
formerly unrecognized disappearance of net worth will become=20
partially recognized (depending upon the extent of such sales) in=20
the insurer=92s published financial statements.

     Variations of this depressing sequence involve a smaller=20
penalty to stated net worth.  The reaction of some companies at=20
(c) would be to sell either stocks that are already carried at=20
market values or recently purchased bonds involving less severe=20
losses.  This ostrich-like behavior - selling the better assets=20
and keeping the biggest losers - while less painful in the short=20
term, is unlikely to be a winner in the long term.

     The second option is much simpler: just keep writing=20
business regardless of rate levels and whopping prospective=20
underwriting losses, thereby maintaining the present levels of=20
premiums, assets and liabilities - and then pray for a better=20
day, either for underwriting or for bond prices.  There is much=20
criticism in the trade press of =93cash flow=94 underwriting; i.e.,=20
writing business regardless of prospective underwriting losses in=20
order to obtain funds to invest at current high interest rates. =20
This second option might properly be termed =93asset maintenance=94=20
underwriting - the acceptance of terrible business just to keep=20
the assets you now have.

     Of course you know which option will be selected.  And it=20
also is clear that as long as many large insurers feel compelled=20
to choose that second option, there will be no better day for=20
underwriting.  For if much of the industry feels it must maintain=20
premium volume levels regardless of price adequacy, all insurers=20
will have to come close to meeting those prices.  Right behind=20
having financial problems yourself, the next worst plight is to=20
have a large group of competitors with financial problems that=20
they can defer by a =93sell-at-any-price=94 policy.

     We mentioned earlier that companies that were unwilling -=20
for any of a number of reasons, including public reaction,=20
institutional pride, or protection of stated net worth - to sell=20
bonds at price levels forcing recognition of major losses might=20
find themselves frozen in investment posture for a decade or=20
longer.  But, as noted, that=92s only half of the problem. =20
<i>Companies that have made extensive commitments to long-term bonds=20
may have lost, for a considerable period of time, not only many=20
of their investment options, but many of their underwriting=20
options as well.</i>

     Our own position in this respect is satisfactory.  We=20
believe our net worth, valuing bonds of all insurers at amortized=20
cost, is the strongest relative to premium volume among all large=20
property-casualty stockholder-owned groups.  When bonds are=20
valued at market, our relative strength becomes far more=20
dramatic. (But lest we get too puffed up, we remind ourselves=20
that our asset and liability maturities still are far more=20
mismatched than we would wish and that we, too, lost important=20
sums in bonds because your Chairman was talking when he should=20
have been acting.)

     Our abundant capital and investment flexibility will enable=20
us to do whatever we think makes the most sense during the=20
prospective extended period of inadequate pricing.  But troubles=20
for the industry mean troubles for us.  Our financial strength=20
doesn=92t remove us from the hostile pricing environment now=20
enveloping the entire property-casualty insurance industry.  It=20
just gives us more staying power and more options.

<i>Insurance Operations</i>

     The National Indemnity managers, led by Phil Liesche with=20
the usual able assistance of Roland Miller and Bill Lyons, outdid=20
themselves in 1980.  While volume was flat, underwriting margins=20
relative to the industry were at an all-time high.  We expect=20
decreased volume from this operation in 1981.  But its managers=20
will hear no complaints from corporate headquarters, nor will=20
employment or salaries suffer.  We enormously admire the National=20
Indemnity underwriting discipline - embedded from origin by the=20
founder, Jack Ringwalt - and know that this discipline, if=20
suspended, probably could not be fully regained.

     John Seward at Home and Auto continues to make good progress=20
in replacing a diminishing number of auto policies with volume=20
from less competitive lines, primarily small-premium general=20
liability.  Operations are being slowly expanded, both=20
geographically and by product line, as warranted by underwriting=20
results.

     The reinsurance business continues to reflect the excesses=20
and problems of the primary writers.  Worse yet, it has the=20
potential for magnifying such excesses.  Reinsurance is=20
characterized by extreme ease of entry, large premium payments in=20
advance, and much-delayed loss reports and loss payments. =20
Initially, the morning mail brings lots of cash and few claims. =20
This state of affairs can produce a blissful, almost euphoric,=20
feeling akin to that experienced by an innocent upon receipt of=20
his first credit card.

     The magnetic lure of such cash-generating characteristics,=20
currently enhanced by the presence of high interest rates, is=20
transforming the reinsurance market into =93amateur night=94. =20
Without a super catastrophe, industry underwriting will be poor=20
in the next few years.  If we experience such a catastrophe,=20
there could be a bloodbath with some companies not able to live=20
up to contractual commitments.  George Young continues to do a=20
first-class job for us in this business.  Results, with=20
investment income included, have been reasonably profitable.  We=20
will retain an active reinsurance presence but, for the=20
foreseeable future, we expect no premium growth from this=20
activity.

     We continue to have serious problems in the Homestate=20
operation.  Floyd Taylor in Kansas has done an outstanding job=20
but our underwriting record elsewhere is considerably below=20
average.  Our poorest performer has been Insurance Company of=20
Iowa, at which large losses have been sustained annually since=20
its founding in 1973.  Late in the fall we abandoned underwriting=20
in that state, and have merged the company into Cornhusker=20
Casualty.  There is potential in the homestate concept, but much=20
work needs to be done in order to realize it.

     Our Workers Compensation operation suffered a severe loss=20
when Frank DeNardo died last year at 37. Frank instinctively=20
thought like an underwriter.  He was a superb technician and a=20
fierce competitor; in short order he had straightened out major=20
problems at the California Workers Compensation Division of=20
National Indemnity.  Dan Grossman, who originally brought Frank=20
to us, stepped in immediately after Frank=92s death to continue=20
that operation, which now utilizes Redwood Fire and Casualty,=20
another Berkshire subsidiary, as the insuring vehicle.

     Our major Workers Compensation operation, Cypress Insurance=20
Company, run by Milt Thornton, continues its outstanding record. =20
Year after year Milt, like Phil Liesche, runs an underwriting=20
operation that far outpaces his competition.  In the industry he=20
is admired and copied, but not matched.

     Overall, we look for a significant decline in insurance=20
volume in 1981 along with a poorer underwriting result.  We=20
expect underwriting experience somewhat superior to that of the=20
industry but, of course, so does most of the industry.  There=20
will be some disappointments.

<i>Textile and Retail Operations</i>

     During the past year we have cut back the scope of our=20
textile business.  Operations at Waumbec Mills have been=20
terminated, reluctantly but necessarily.  Some equipment was=20
transferred to New Bedford but most has been sold, or will be,=20
along with real estate.  Your Chairman made a costly mistake in=20
not facing the realities of this situation sooner.

     At New Bedford we have reduced the number of looms operated=20
by about one-third, abandoning some high-volume lines in which=20
product differentiation was insignificant.  Even assuming=20
everything went right - which it seldom did - these lines could=20
not generate adequate returns related to investment.  And, over a=20
full industry cycle, losses were the most likely result.

     Our remaining textile operation, still sizable, has been=20
divided into a manufacturing and a sales division, each free to=20
do business independent of the other.  Thus, distribution=20
strengths and mill capabilities will not be wedded to each other. =20
We have more than doubled capacity in our most profitable textile=20
segment through a recent purchase of used 130-inch Saurer looms. =20
Current conditions indicate another tough year in textiles, but=20
with substantially less capital employed in the operation.

     Ben Rosner=92s record at Associated Retail Stores continues to=20
amaze us.  In a poor retailing year, Associated=92s earnings=20
continued excellent - and those earnings all were translated into=20
cash.  On March 7, 1981 Associated will celebrate its 50th=20
birthday.  Ben has run the business (along with Leo Simon, his=20
partner from 1931 to 1966) in each of those fifty years.

<i>Disposition of Illinois National Bank and Trust of Rockford</i>

     On December 31, 1980 we completed the exchange of 41,086=20
shares of Rockford Bancorp Inc. (which owns 97.7% of Illinois=20
National Bank) for a like number of shares of Berkshire Hathaway=20
Inc.

     Our method of exchange allowed all Berkshire shareholders to=20
maintain their proportional interest in the Bank (except for me;=20
I was permitted 80% of my proportional share).  They were thus=20
guaranteed an ownership position identical to that they would=20
have attained had we followed a more conventional spinoff=20
approach.  Twenty-four shareholders (of our approximate 1300)=20
chose this proportional exchange option.

     We also allowed overexchanges, and thirty-nine additional=20
shareholders accepted this option, thereby increasing their=20
ownership in the Bank and decreasing their proportional ownership=20
in Berkshire.  All got the full amount of Bancorp stock they=20
requested, since the total shares desired by these thirty-nine=20
holders was just slightly less than the number left available by=20
the remaining 1200-plus holders of Berkshire who elected not to=20
part with any Berkshire shares at all.  As the exchanger of last=20
resort, I took the small balance (3% of Bancorp=92s stock).  These=20
shares, added to shares I received from my basic exchange=20
allotment (80% of normal), gave me a slightly reduced=20
proportional interest in the Bank and a slightly enlarged=20
proportional interest in Berkshire.

     Management of the Bank is pleased with the outcome.  Bancorp=20
will operate as an inexpensive and uncomplicated holding company=20
owned by 65 shareholders.  And all of those shareholders will=20
have become Bancorp owners through a conscious affirmative=20
decision.

<i>Financing</i>

     In August we sold $60 million of 12 3/4% notes due August 1,=20
2005, with a sinking fund to begin in 1991.

     The managing underwriters, Donaldson, Lufkin &amp; Jenrette=20
Securities Corporation, represented by Bill Fisher, and Chiles,=20
Heider &amp; Company, Inc., represented by Charlie Heider, did an=20
absolutely first-class job from start to finish of the financing.

     Unlike most businesses, Berkshire did not finance because of=20
any specific immediate needs.  Rather, we borrowed because we=20
think that, over a period far shorter than the life of the loan,=20
we will have many opportunities to put the money to good use. =20
The most attractive opportunities may present themselves at a=20
time when credit is extremely expensive - or even unavailable. =20
At such a time we want to have plenty of financial firepower.

     Our acquisition preferences run toward businesses that=20
generate cash, not those that consume it.  As inflation=20
intensifies, more and more companies find that they must spend=20
all funds they generate internally just to maintain their=20
existing physical volume of business.  There is a certain mirage-
like quality to such operations.  However attractive the earnings=20
numbers, we remain leery of businesses that never seem able to=20
convert such pretty numbers into no-strings-attached cash.

     Businesses meeting our standards are not easy to find. (Each=20
year we read of hundreds of corporate acquisitions; only a=20
handful would have been of interest to us.) And logical expansion=20
of our present operations is not easy to implement.  But we=92ll=20
continue to utilize both avenues in our attempts to further=20
Berkshire=92s growth.

     Under all circumstances we plan to operate with plenty of=20
liquidity, with debt that is moderate in size and properly=20
structured, and with an abundance of capital strength.  Our=20
return on equity is penalized somewhat by this conservative=20
approach, but it is the only one with which we feel comfortable.


               *  *  *  *  *  *  *  *  *  *  *  *               =20


     Gene Abegg, founder of our long-owned bank in Rockford, died=20
on July 2, 1980 at the age of 82.  As a friend, banker and=20
citizen, he was unsurpassed.

     You learn a great deal about a person when you purchase a=20
business from him and he then stays on to run it as an employee=20
rather than as an owner.  Before the purchase the seller knows=20
the business intimately, whereas you start from scratch.  The=20
seller has dozens of opportunities to mislead the buyer - through=20
omissions, ambiguities, and misdirection.  After the check has=20
changed hands, subtle (and not so subtle) changes of attitude can=20
occur and implicit understandings can evaporate.  As in the=20
courtship-marriage sequence, disappointments are not infrequent.

     From the time we first met, Gene shot straight 100% of the=20
time - the only behavior pattern he had within him.  At the=20
outset of negotiations, he laid all negative factors face up on=20
the table; on the other hand, for years after the transaction was=20
completed he would tell me periodically of some previously=20
undiscussed items of value that had come with our purchase.

     Though he was already 71 years of age when he sold us the=20
Bank, Gene subsequently worked harder for us than he had for=20
himself.  He never delayed reporting a problem for a minute, but=20
problems were few with Gene.  What else would you expect from a=20
man who, at the time of the bank holiday in 1933, had enough cash=20
on the premises to pay all depositors in full?  Gene never forgot=20
he was handling other people=92s money.  Though this fiduciary=20
attitude was always dominant, his superb managerial skills=20
enabled the Bank to regularly achieve the top position nationally=20
in profitability.

     Gene was in charge of the Illinois National for close to=20
fifty years - almost one-quarter of the lifetime of our country. =20
George Mead, a wealthy industrialist, brought him in from Chicago=20
to open a new bank after a number of other banks in Rockford had=20
failed.  Mr. Mead put up the money and Gene ran the show.  His=20
talent for leadership soon put its stamp on virtually every major=20
civic activity in Rockford.

     Dozens of Rockford citizens have told me over the years of=20
help Gene extended to them.  In some cases this help was=20
financial; in all cases it involved much wisdom, empathy and=20
friendship.  He always offered the same to me.  Because of our=20
respective ages and positions I was sometimes the junior partner,=20
sometimes the senior.  Whichever the relationship, it always was=20
a special one, and I miss it.


                                          Warren E. Buffett
February 27, 1981                         Chairman of the Board

